
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">As we all experiment with AI and how it can help, I went down a rabbit hole over the last month that I wanted to share. I called it &#8220;The Daybook&#8221;. It was an automated daily note. It ran every night, pulling in my calendar, my open tasks, my repeating chores, and anything else I might need before the day started. It was pretty advanced and I enjoyed building it.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">But I found myself not looking at it. So I tinkered with it. And I still didn&#8217;t read it. The assembly was never the problem. By the time that page existed the thinking was already done, and somebody (or some <em>thing</em>) else had done it.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A note handed to you is something to review. A note you wrote yourself is something you already know. I realized that writing the list is how I find out what&#8217;s on it.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For a long time I&#8217;ve had an analog version of the Daybook. It&#8217;s a <em>Field Notes</em> notebook I carry around with me. The technology is wood and graphite and paper. The process is full of glorious friction and my Daybook experiment proved I actually need that friction in my life.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">So I retired the Daybook. There is a lot that my computer can help me with. It just turns out I need to manage my life for myself. If I want to know what matters today, I have to be the one who writes it down.</p>



